{"type":"rewrite","text":"Independent study has become increasingly important for university students. It allows learners to arrange their own time, choose materials that suit their needs, and develop the ability to solve problems on their own. Without the pressure of a traditional classroom, students can focus on their weak areas and learn at their own pace. In this sense, studying alone is a valuable skill that prepares students for lifelong learning.\n\nHowever, independent study does not mean that students should work completely alone. Feedback is also useful, because it helps learners discover mistakes that they may not notice by themselves. Teachers can offer professional advice, while classmates can share different ideas and experiences. With timely feedback, students can adjust their learning methods and avoid repeating the same errors. Therefore, independent study and feedback should not be seen as opposites.\n\nIn conclusion, students can study independently, but they should actively seek feedback. A balance between working alone and learning from others will make their study more effective and help them achieve better results."}

